1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,463
Computational Skepticism, Exam Prep.

2
00:00:01,513 --> 00:00:03,355
Key Terms: Visualization Under Validation.

3
00:00:03,405 --> 00:00:08,652
Thirty-two terms for building and checking
a chart, from how a dashboard argues to

4
00:00:08,702 --> 00:00:11,300
how uncertainty, axes and summaries can
mislead.

5
00:00:11,350 --> 00:00:15,840
Knowing the names lets you catch a
misleading chart, including your own.

6
00:00:16,990 --> 00:00:17,197
Hallo.

7
00:00:17,247 --> 00:00:18,736
This is Liam, in for Bear.

8
00:00:18,786 --> 00:00:21,814
Most people think a chart is a clear view
of the data.

9
00:00:21,864 --> 00:00:22,584
It is not.

10
00:00:22,634 --> 00:00:26,689
A chart is an argument about the data,
made by the layout, the axes, the colors,

11
00:00:26,739 --> 00:00:27,972
and what gets left off.

12
00:00:28,022 --> 00:00:31,563
This film goes through thirty-two terms
for those choices, each one shown on a

13
00:00:31,613 --> 00:00:31,820
picture.

14
00:00:32,170 --> 00:00:35,256
Five labels sit in the corners of the
pictures that follow.

15
00:00:35,306 --> 00:00:38,677
In the book text: the course text uses and
explains the term.

16
00:00:38,727 --> 00:00:41,812
Computed: the numbers are worked out on
screen from stated inputs.

17
00:00:41,862 --> 00:00:46,374
Simulation: a made-up run with a fixed
seed, so it comes out the same every time.

18
00:00:46,424 --> 00:00:49,224
Illustrative data: numbers chosen to show
the idea, not measured.

19
00:00:49,274 --> 00:00:52,360
Conceptual: a drawing of the idea, with no
data behind it.

20
00:00:52,710 --> 00:00:56,028
This is a map of the terms, thirty-two of
them in four groups.

21
00:00:56,078 --> 00:00:57,842
The first group is Argument and channel.

22
00:00:57,892 --> 00:01:01,210
It covers why a chart is a claim, and how
to pick one.

23
00:01:01,560 --> 00:01:03,469
The second group is Showing uncertainty.

24
00:01:03,519 --> 00:01:08,694
It covers what the spread and the doubt
look like on a chart, from aleatoric and

25
00:01:08,744 --> 00:01:11,960
epistemic uncertainty to error bars, the
cone, and false precision.

26
00:01:12,310 --> 00:01:14,416
The third group is Axes and summaries.

27
00:01:14,466 --> 00:01:18,729
These are ten moves that mislead, such as
a truncated axis, a cherry-picked window,

28
00:01:18,779 --> 00:01:19,961
or a missing baseline.

29
00:01:20,011 --> 00:01:23,350
Each also has an honest use, so you need
the name.

30
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:25,723
The last group is Building honestly.

31
00:01:25,773 --> 00:01:29,869
These are practices that keep a chart
accountable, the living deck, its

32
00:01:29,919 --> 00:01:32,979
changelog, the evidence-grade chart, and
the non-author reader test.

33
00:01:33,029 --> 00:01:36,780
Every term here is a choice that moves the
reader's conclusion.

34
00:01:37,130 --> 00:01:40,832
A dashboard from a made-up example, with
ninety-four percent accuracy in large type

35
00:01:40,882 --> 00:01:41,409
on top.

36
00:01:41,459 --> 00:01:45,449
Dashboard as argument means the layout
makes a case before anyone takes in a

37
00:01:45,499 --> 00:01:45,738
number.

38
00:01:45,788 --> 00:01:48,624
Here only one of three findings shows at
first glance.

39
00:01:48,674 --> 00:01:52,375
For example, a job-search agent's weekly
report puts applications sent in huge type

40
00:01:52,425 --> 00:01:56,127
at the top, and the replies in a small
table at the bottom.

41
00:01:56,177 --> 00:01:59,590
The layout says the week went well before
anyone checks the replies.

42
00:01:59,940 --> 00:02:01,627
The same data in equal panels.

43
00:02:01,677 --> 00:02:05,390
Medium is the message, McLuhan's phrase,
means the form shapes the meaning before

44
00:02:05,440 --> 00:02:06,258
the details arrive.

45
00:02:06,308 --> 00:02:08,863
Equal panels tell the reader to weigh
everything together.

46
00:02:08,913 --> 00:02:11,179
Now all three findings show at first
glance.

47
00:02:11,229 --> 00:02:15,810
For example, take the same work history
sent as a tight résumé or as a long

48
00:02:15,860 --> 00:02:16,389
scrolling portfolio.

49
00:02:16,439 --> 00:02:20,731
Before a recruiter takes in a single job
title, the form already says focused, or

50
00:02:20,781 --> 00:02:21,310
says scattered.

51
00:02:21,660 --> 00:02:23,444
Both dashboards, from one data file.

52
00:02:23,494 --> 00:02:28,028
Every number is true, yet on the first,
two of three findings never reach the

53
00:02:28,078 --> 00:02:28,334
reader.

54
00:02:28,384 --> 00:02:31,696
That is an Output-layer failure, a
validation failure, not a communication

55
00:02:31,746 --> 00:02:32,612
problem for later.

56
00:02:32,662 --> 00:02:36,891
For example, an agent correctly finds that
a job seeker gets interviews at small

57
00:02:36,941 --> 00:02:39,031
firms and almost none at large ones.

58
00:02:39,081 --> 00:02:43,309
Its chart shows only the overall interview
rate, so the seeker keeps applying to

59
00:02:43,359 --> 00:02:43,921
large firms.

60
00:02:43,971 --> 00:02:45,143
The analysis was right.

61
00:02:45,193 --> 00:02:46,060
The chart failed.

62
00:02:46,410 --> 00:02:49,614
A grid of nine question families, each
with a typical chart.

63
00:02:49,664 --> 00:02:52,572
This is the FT question taxonomy, FT for
Financial Times.

64
00:02:52,622 --> 00:02:55,530
State the question, find its family, then
pick the chart.

65
00:02:55,580 --> 00:02:59,672
For example, a job seeker asks how their
reply rate changed month by month.

66
00:02:59,722 --> 00:03:02,039
That is change over time, so a line.

67
00:03:02,089 --> 00:03:06,180
Asking which job board brings the most
interviews is ranking, so an ordered bar.

68
00:03:06,530 --> 00:03:09,300
A ranked list of visual channels, most
accurate first.

69
00:03:09,350 --> 00:03:10,867
This is the Perception hierarchy.

70
00:03:10,917 --> 00:03:14,941
Readers judge position on a common scale
best, then length, angle, area, volume,

71
00:03:14,991 --> 00:03:15,881
and color last.

72
00:03:15,931 --> 00:03:19,015
Color suits categories, and is weak for
showing how much.

73
00:03:19,065 --> 00:03:23,089
For example, a job-market report draws
demand for each skill as circles of

74
00:03:23,139 --> 00:03:23,716
different size.

75
00:03:23,766 --> 00:03:24,656
Readers misjudge areas.

76
00:03:24,706 --> 00:03:28,730
The same numbers as bars of different
length are compared far more accurately.

77
00:03:29,080 --> 00:03:31,949
The same made-up shares as a pie and as
dots.

78
00:03:31,999 --> 00:03:35,744
Common-scale position means values shown
by where points sit on one shared axis.

79
00:03:35,794 --> 00:03:38,662
On the dots, A is plainly largest at
twenty-three percent.

80
00:03:38,712 --> 00:03:40,414
On the pie, ranking is hard.

81
00:03:40,464 --> 00:03:44,208
For example, plot the share of a job
seeker's applications sent through each

82
00:03:44,258 --> 00:03:47,711
job board as dots on one shared axis, not
as pie slices.

83
00:03:47,761 --> 00:03:50,630
Which board got the most is plain at a
glance.

84
00:03:50,980 --> 00:03:53,735
Thirty polls of one population, from a
simulated run.

85
00:03:53,785 --> 00:03:57,164
Their scatter around the dashed line is
Aleatoric uncertainty, variation from

86
00:03:57,214 --> 00:03:59,346
sampling and chance that cannot be
removed.

87
00:03:59,396 --> 00:04:02,775
The shaded band is the margin, about three
points each way.

88
00:04:02,825 --> 00:04:07,139
For example, a job seeker sends the same
résumé to similar listings in different

89
00:04:07,189 --> 00:04:07,451
weeks.

90
00:04:07,501 --> 00:04:09,944
One week brings several replies, another
brings few.

91
00:04:09,994 --> 00:04:11,503
Nothing about the résumé changed.

92
00:04:11,553 --> 00:04:14,620
That spread is chance, and an honest chart
shows it.

93
00:04:14,970 --> 00:04:17,948
Two poll bars, forty-five point three
percent against forty-four point five.

94
00:04:17,998 --> 00:04:21,526
The gap, zero point eight, is inside the
margin, two point nine five.

95
00:04:21,576 --> 00:04:24,278
That is Uncertainty over effect, so call
it a tie.

96
00:04:24,328 --> 00:04:25,655
The poll's source is unverified.

97
00:04:25,705 --> 00:04:29,783
For example, an agent tests a new cover
letter against the old one on small

98
00:04:29,833 --> 00:04:30,609
batches of applications.

99
00:04:30,659 --> 00:04:35,013
The new one gets a slightly higher reply
rate, but the margin of error is wider

100
00:04:35,063 --> 00:04:35,839
than the gap.

101
00:04:35,889 --> 00:04:37,490
Report a tie, not a winner.

102
00:04:37,840 --> 00:04:39,212
Two lines from made-up data.

103
00:04:39,262 --> 00:04:41,203
News stories climb, the events stay flat.

104
00:04:41,253 --> 00:04:45,184
Epistemic uncertainty is the gap between
what is known and what is true, from

105
00:04:45,234 --> 00:04:46,891
limits of data, instruments and proxies.

106
00:04:46,941 --> 00:04:48,882
It is doubt about the data itself.

107
00:04:48,932 --> 00:04:52,579
For example, a job-market report counts
job postings as a stand-in for hiring.

108
00:04:52,629 --> 00:04:57,129
But a posting is not a hire, and some
postings stay up with no one hired.

109
00:04:57,179 --> 00:04:59,120
Postings can climb while hiring stays
flat.

110
00:04:59,470 --> 00:05:02,397
The same two indexes as a scatter, news
against events.

111
00:05:02,447 --> 00:05:06,267
The correlation is zero point one zero, so
the proxy fails the check.

112
00:05:06,317 --> 00:05:09,840
Validate first means checking that the
data measures what the question asks

113
00:05:09,890 --> 00:05:11,328
before drawing anything from it.

114
00:05:11,378 --> 00:05:15,496
For example, before charting an agent's
fit scores to guide where to apply, check

115
00:05:15,546 --> 00:05:16,687
them against what happened.

116
00:05:16,737 --> 00:05:18,771
Did high-scoring listings actually bring
more interviews?

117
00:05:18,821 --> 00:05:21,450
If not, the score fails, and the chart
waits.

118
00:05:21,800 --> 00:05:23,720
Three simulated estimates, each with red
whiskers.

119
00:05:23,770 --> 00:05:26,534
These are Error bars, a point estimate
with its spread.

120
00:05:26,584 --> 00:05:30,474
The chart must say what the bar is, here a
ninety-five percent CI, confidence

121
00:05:30,524 --> 00:05:30,756
interval.

122
00:05:30,806 --> 00:05:32,726
Only A and C do not overlap.

123
00:05:32,776 --> 00:05:36,947
For example, a salary tool shows a
predicted salary for each job title with a

124
00:05:36,997 --> 00:05:37,791
bar around it.

125
00:05:37,841 --> 00:05:41,731
Is the bar the range of real salaries, or
the doubt in the average?

126
00:05:41,781 --> 00:05:43,420
Unlabeled, the job seeker cannot tell.

127
00:05:43,770 --> 00:05:45,097
The same three estimates, twice.

128
00:05:45,147 --> 00:05:48,403
On the left every interval is drawn, and A
and B overlap.

129
00:05:48,453 --> 00:05:51,985
On the right B has no interval, so B looks
plainly above A.

130
00:05:52,035 --> 00:05:54,464
Selective uncertainty is showing intervals
only where they help.

131
00:05:54,514 --> 00:05:57,770
For example, a vendor's report on its
résumé-screening tool draws an interval

132
00:05:57,820 --> 00:06:01,627
where the tool clearly beats the old
process, and leaves it off where the

133
00:06:01,677 --> 00:06:03,280
tool's edge is inside the noise.

134
00:06:03,630 --> 00:06:06,905
A trend line with a pale band behind it,
on illustrative data.

135
00:06:06,955 --> 00:06:09,952
Interval bands show uncertainty as a
shaded region around a line.

136
00:06:10,002 --> 00:06:12,446
Pale shading carries the width, so readers
misjudge it.

137
00:06:12,496 --> 00:06:14,385
So label the width where it matters.

138
00:06:14,435 --> 00:06:18,541
For example, a fitted trend of weekly
postings for data jobs, drawn as a line

139
00:06:18,591 --> 00:06:21,034
with a pale band that widens in later
weeks.

140
00:06:21,084 --> 00:06:25,190
Faint shading hides that widening, so
label how wide the band is at the end.

141
00:06:25,540 --> 00:06:28,598
A forecast cone with simulated errors
stacked at its wide end.

142
00:06:28,648 --> 00:06:32,836
The Cone of uncertainty is sized to hold
about two thirds of past track errors.

143
00:06:32,886 --> 00:06:36,509
About a third fall outside, and the cone
is not the storm's size.

144
00:06:36,559 --> 00:06:40,747
For example, a job-market report projects
openings in a field as a cone that widens

145
00:06:40,797 --> 00:06:41,877
over the coming years.

146
00:06:41,927 --> 00:06:44,420
Its width comes from how wrong past
projections were.

147
00:06:44,470 --> 00:06:47,810
A wide cone means doubt about the
forecast, not a bigger market.

148
00:06:48,160 --> 00:06:50,539
Twenty dots on a scale of delay in
minutes.

149
00:06:50,589 --> 00:06:52,968
This Quantile dotplot shows a probability
as a count.

150
00:06:53,018 --> 00:06:55,936
Four of the twenty dots, in red, sit
beyond forty-five minutes.

151
00:06:55,986 --> 00:06:58,905
That is a twenty percent chance, and the
reader just counts.

152
00:06:58,955 --> 00:07:01,873
For example, an agent estimates how long
until a first interview.

153
00:07:01,923 --> 00:07:05,921
Instead of a percentage, it shows twenty
dots of possible waits, and the job seeker

154
00:07:05,971 --> 00:07:08,890
counts how many fall past the week their
savings run out.

155
00:07:09,240 --> 00:07:11,030
One thousand people as a grid.

156
00:07:11,080 --> 00:07:13,791
Ten have the condition, nine of them test
positive.

157
00:07:13,841 --> 00:07:15,631
Ninety-nine of the others do too.

158
00:07:15,681 --> 00:07:18,698
So only nine of one hundred eight
positives are real.

159
00:07:18,748 --> 00:07:21,152
Natural frequencies state probabilities as
counts like these.

160
00:07:21,202 --> 00:07:25,445
For example, rather than calling a résumé
filter highly accurate, say it in counts.

161
00:07:25,495 --> 00:07:29,433
Of every thousand applicants, this many
are qualified and get flagged, and this

162
00:07:29,483 --> 00:07:31,273
many unqualified ones get flagged too.

163
00:07:31,323 --> 00:07:34,340
A recruiter can then count how many flags
are real.

164
00:07:34,690 --> 00:07:35,758
One result, two ways.

165
00:07:35,808 --> 00:07:39,390
On the left, a huge ninety-four point
three percent over a thin line.

166
00:07:39,440 --> 00:07:42,742
On the right, the range, ninety-one to
ninety-seven percent, at full size.

167
00:07:42,792 --> 00:07:45,815
Equal visual weight draws the uncertainty
as prominently as the estimate.

168
00:07:45,865 --> 00:07:50,285
For example, a salary tool shows an offer
estimate in huge type, with a faint range

169
00:07:50,335 --> 00:07:50,844
beneath it.

170
00:07:50,894 --> 00:07:55,594
Drawn at equal weight, the low and high
ends sit as large as the middle, and the

171
00:07:55,644 --> 00:07:57,270
job seeker negotiates knowing the spread.

172
00:07:57,620 --> 00:08:00,987
A long decimal, struck out, rounded to
zero point eight five.

173
00:08:01,037 --> 00:08:04,094
False precision is reporting more digits
than the uncertainty supports.

174
00:08:04,144 --> 00:08:08,444
The margin is zero point zero five, so
digits finer than hundredths imply a

175
00:08:08,494 --> 00:08:09,997
certainty that is not there.

176
00:08:10,047 --> 00:08:13,725
For example, an agent rates a listing's
fit to many decimal places.

177
00:08:13,775 --> 00:08:18,385
Reword one line of the résumé and the
score shifts in its first decimal place.

178
00:08:18,435 --> 00:08:20,560
Every digit past that is false precision.

179
00:08:20,910 --> 00:08:22,254
Two bars on two axes.

180
00:08:22,304 --> 00:08:25,600
On an axis from ninety-one to ninety-six,
B looks three times A.

181
00:08:25,650 --> 00:08:28,388
From zero, the real ratio is one point
zero two.

182
00:08:28,438 --> 00:08:31,454
Truncated axes start above zero, so a
small difference looks large.

183
00:08:31,504 --> 00:08:35,915
For example, a report compares interview
rates for an old and a new résumé on an

184
00:08:35,965 --> 00:08:37,588
axis that starts just below both.

185
00:08:37,638 --> 00:08:39,818
A tiny gap looks like a big win.

186
00:08:39,868 --> 00:08:41,770
From zero, the bars are nearly equal.

187
00:08:42,120 --> 00:08:45,654
Three panels with their own axes on top,
one shared axis below.

188
00:08:45,704 --> 00:08:49,834
Group B has the highest value, ninety, yet
on its own axis looks lowest.

189
00:08:49,884 --> 00:08:53,418
Inconsistent axes are panels with
different ranges, so comparing by eye is

190
00:08:53,468 --> 00:08:54,015
not valid.

191
00:08:54,065 --> 00:08:57,300
For example, side-by-side panels show
reply rates from different job boards,

192
00:08:57,350 --> 00:08:59,092
each scaled to its own range.

193
00:08:59,142 --> 00:09:02,078
The board with the best rate can look the
worst.

194
00:09:02,128 --> 00:09:03,870
One shared axis fixes the comparison.

195
00:09:04,220 --> 00:09:07,407
One curve, on a linear axis and on a log
axis.

196
00:09:07,457 --> 00:09:10,939
Scale trickery is a linear axis for
naturally logarithmic data, or the

197
00:09:10,989 --> 00:09:12,705
reverse, changing which region looks big.

198
00:09:12,755 --> 00:09:14,471
Linear shows the size of gains.

199
00:09:14,521 --> 00:09:15,354
Log shows ratios.

200
00:09:15,404 --> 00:09:19,474
For example, chart how many applicants
each job posting draws, from a handful to

201
00:09:19,524 --> 00:09:20,062
many thousands.

202
00:09:20,112 --> 00:09:24,771
On a linear axis the few huge postings
fill the chart and the rest flatten near

203
00:09:24,821 --> 00:09:25,066
zero.

204
00:09:25,116 --> 00:09:27,420
On a log axis every posting gets room.

205
00:09:27,770 --> 00:09:29,253
Three yearly counts, drawn twice.

206
00:09:29,303 --> 00:09:32,931
Firearm murders in Florida rose from five
hundred twenty-one to eight hundred

207
00:09:32,981 --> 00:09:33,238
twenty-five.

208
00:09:33,288 --> 00:09:36,609
With zero at the top, that rise draws as a
fall.

209
00:09:36,659 --> 00:09:37,835
That is Axis inversion.

210
00:09:37,885 --> 00:09:39,981
Truncation stretches a change, inversion
reverses it.

211
00:09:40,031 --> 00:09:44,579
For example, a chart of a job seeker's
rejections per month, drawn with zero at

212
00:09:44,629 --> 00:09:45,192
the top.

213
00:09:45,242 --> 00:09:49,790
As rejections pile up the line drops, so a
bad stretch looks like an improvement.

214
00:09:50,140 --> 00:09:53,720
One thousand simulated response times,
with the median at zero point five

215
00:09:53,770 --> 00:09:54,023
seconds.

216
00:09:54,073 --> 00:09:57,351
In red, the slowest three percent reach
seven point seven seconds.

217
00:09:57,401 --> 00:10:01,586
Hiding the distribution means a mean or
median that conceals a long tail or

218
00:10:01,636 --> 00:10:02,191
several clusters.

219
00:10:02,241 --> 00:10:06,729
For example, a report gives the median
wait to hear back after applying, and it

220
00:10:06,779 --> 00:10:07,334
is short.

221
00:10:07,384 --> 00:10:10,057
Hidden in the tail are applications that
waited months.

222
00:10:10,107 --> 00:10:15,200
The median is true, and it hides the waits
a job seeker most needs to plan for.

223
00:10:15,550 --> 00:10:16,357
Monthly accuracy, illustrative.

224
00:10:16,407 --> 00:10:20,929
The shaded window starts after the drop
and ends at the high, with a fitted trend

225
00:10:20,979 --> 00:10:23,215
of plus one point eight points a month.

226
00:10:23,265 --> 00:10:25,502
From January it is plus zero point five.

227
00:10:25,552 --> 00:10:26,930
That is a Cherry-picked window.

228
00:10:26,980 --> 00:10:30,645
For example, a career bootcamp charts its
graduates' interview rate from its worst

229
00:10:30,695 --> 00:10:31,788
month to its best.

230
00:10:31,838 --> 00:10:34,360
Over the whole period, the climb is far
smaller.

231
00:10:34,710 --> 00:10:37,573
A model at ninety-four percent looks like
a finding.

232
00:10:37,623 --> 00:10:41,133
Beside it, always guessing negative scores
ninety-three percent in this made-up

233
00:10:41,183 --> 00:10:41,456
example.

234
00:10:41,506 --> 00:10:43,075
The lift is one point.

235
00:10:43,125 --> 00:10:47,282
A Missing baseline is a metric shown
without the comparison that gives it

236
00:10:47,332 --> 00:10:47,605
meaning.

237
00:10:47,655 --> 00:10:51,165
For example, an agent boasts that it
predicts most application outcomes

238
00:10:51,215 --> 00:10:51,489
correctly.

239
00:10:51,539 --> 00:10:55,373
But most applications are rejected anyway,
so always guessing rejection scores nearly

240
00:10:55,423 --> 00:10:56,020
as well.

241
00:10:56,370 --> 00:10:57,750
The same scores, drawn twice.

242
00:10:57,800 --> 00:10:59,753
On the left every bar is dark.

243
00:10:59,803 --> 00:11:02,899
On the right the three poorest groups are
pale and recede.

244
00:11:02,949 --> 00:11:06,618
Color asymmetry means colors of unequal
salience that steer the eye toward some

245
00:11:06,668 --> 00:11:07,762
categories, away from others.

246
00:11:07,812 --> 00:11:11,481
For example, a hiring-funnel chart shows
hired applicants in bold color and the

247
00:11:11,531 --> 00:11:13,769
groups the résumé filter dropped in pale
gray.

248
00:11:13,819 --> 00:11:18,060
The counts are all there, but the eye
skips the people who were screened out.

249
00:11:18,410 --> 00:11:20,990
Two equal values, drawn flat and then in
three-D.

250
00:11:21,040 --> 00:11:23,035
In three-D the far bar looks smaller.

251
00:11:23,085 --> 00:11:26,833
Chartjunk and three-D means decoration and
depth effects that carry no data, and

252
00:11:26,883 --> 00:11:28,586
still distort how big values look.

253
00:11:28,636 --> 00:11:32,384
For example, an agent's weekly summary
shows applications per job board as a

254
00:11:32,434 --> 00:11:33,261
tilted three-D pie.

255
00:11:33,311 --> 00:11:38,228
The front slice looks bigger than an equal
slice at the back, and the tilt carries no

256
00:11:38,278 --> 00:11:38,520
data.

257
00:11:38,870 --> 00:11:40,427
Two bars under two titles.

258
00:11:40,477 --> 00:11:44,285
One says accuracy boost from new model, a
conclusion before the data.

259
00:11:44,335 --> 00:11:46,213
The other just names the variables.

260
00:11:46,263 --> 00:11:49,749
Labels that prejudge are titles and legend
entries that argue first.

261
00:11:49,799 --> 00:11:51,678
Chartjunk decorates, a prejudging label
argues.

262
00:11:51,728 --> 00:11:55,535
For example, a chart titled AI-tailored
résumés win more interviews, over two

263
00:11:55,585 --> 00:11:56,500
nearly equal bars.

264
00:11:56,550 --> 00:12:01,000
A plain title, interview rate by résumé
version, lets the data make the argument.

265
00:12:01,350 --> 00:12:05,811
A row of numbered pages with the second to
last marked changelog, above a made-up

266
00:12:05,861 --> 00:12:06,412
version log.

267
00:12:06,462 --> 00:12:10,923
A Living deck is a presentation that shows
its own dated version history instead of

268
00:12:10,973 --> 00:12:11,825
posing as finished.

269
00:12:11,875 --> 00:12:15,434
Its Changelog slide lists what changed
since the last version, and why.

270
00:12:15,484 --> 00:12:20,245
For example, a job seeker keeps their
search plan as a living deck to share with

271
00:12:20,295 --> 00:12:20,846
a mentor.

272
00:12:20,896 --> 00:12:25,056
Its Changelog slide notes that the target
list now includes remote roles, and why,

273
00:12:25,106 --> 00:12:26,560
because local listings dried up.

274
00:12:26,910 --> 00:12:29,119
A simulated map, six pumps and forty-five
deaths.

275
00:12:29,169 --> 00:12:30,531
Thirty-three fall nearest one pump.

276
00:12:30,581 --> 00:12:34,202
An Evidence-grade chart shows what would
make the conclusion wrong, not only what

277
00:12:34,252 --> 00:12:34,766
supports it.

278
00:12:34,816 --> 00:12:38,155
Every pump is drawn, so the reader can
rule out the rest.

279
00:12:38,205 --> 00:12:41,826
For example, to claim that tailored cover
letters bring more interviews, also chart

280
00:12:41,876 --> 00:12:45,496
the applications sent without one, and the
roles where tailoring made no difference.

281
00:12:45,546 --> 00:12:48,320
The reader can see what would prove the
claim wrong.

282
00:12:48,670 --> 00:12:52,010
Two bars, and five readers' made-up
guesses of how much bigger B looks.

283
00:12:52,060 --> 00:12:53,574
All five disagree with the data.

284
00:12:53,624 --> 00:12:57,486
The Non-author reader test asks someone
who did not make the chart what it says.

285
00:12:57,536 --> 00:12:59,050
A mismatch means the chart fails.

286
00:12:59,100 --> 00:13:03,223
For example, before an agent's funnel
chart goes to a job seeker, show it to a

287
00:13:03,273 --> 00:13:04,787
friend who did not build it.

288
00:13:04,837 --> 00:13:08,698
If the friend says the search is going
well while the replies say otherwise, the

289
00:13:08,748 --> 00:13:09,220
chart fails.

290
00:13:09,570 --> 00:13:13,229
Two sets of dots, each beside a dashed
line for the true value.

291
00:13:13,279 --> 00:13:14,370
On the left, Aleatoric.

292
00:13:14,420 --> 00:13:18,079
The dots scatter around the truth,
variation that is really in the data.

293
00:13:18,129 --> 00:13:19,220
On the right, Epistemic.

294
00:13:19,270 --> 00:13:23,785
The dots are tight but sit off to one
side, doubt about whether the data is

295
00:13:23,835 --> 00:13:24,070
right.

296
00:13:24,420 --> 00:13:26,102
On the left, a Truncated axis.

297
00:13:26,152 --> 00:13:30,144
It starts at eighty, so eighty-two against
eighty-five looks two and a half times

298
00:13:30,194 --> 00:13:30,433
bigger.

299
00:13:30,483 --> 00:13:31,588
It amplifies a gap.

300
00:13:31,638 --> 00:13:36,785
On the right, an Inverted axis, with zero
at the top, so a rise draws as a fall.

301
00:13:36,835 --> 00:13:37,940
It reverses a trend.

302
00:13:38,290 --> 00:13:40,356
Two error bars that mean different things.

303
00:13:40,406 --> 00:13:44,890
On the left the bar is a standard
deviation, plus or minus ten, the spread

304
00:13:44,940 --> 00:13:46,099
of the individual values.

305
00:13:46,149 --> 00:13:50,935
On the right the bar is a ninety-five
percent CI, plus or minus three point nine

306
00:13:50,985 --> 00:13:53,051
two, the uncertainty in the estimated
mean.

307
00:13:53,101 --> 00:13:54,260
So label the bar.

308
00:13:54,610 --> 00:13:55,490
Two small maps.

309
00:13:55,540 --> 00:13:57,040
On the left, an Illustration.

310
00:13:57,090 --> 00:14:01,690
Only the pump under suspicion is drawn, so
it shows only what supports the claim.

311
00:14:01,740 --> 00:14:03,550
On the right, an Evidence-grade chart.

312
00:14:03,600 --> 00:14:07,890
All the pumps are drawn, so it also shows
what could refute the claim.

313
00:14:08,240 --> 00:14:09,739
Vague requests, each made exact.

314
00:14:09,789 --> 00:14:11,287
First, make the chart clear.

315
00:14:11,337 --> 00:14:16,553
Say instead, start bar axes at zero, or
mark the cut and say why it is safe.

316
00:14:16,603 --> 00:14:20,580
A bar that looked three times bigger
becomes one point zero two times.

317
00:14:20,930 --> 00:14:22,142
Next, show the uncertainty.

318
00:14:22,192 --> 00:14:27,188
Say instead, draw plus or minus two point
nine five on both poll shares at equal

319
00:14:27,238 --> 00:14:27,504
weight.

320
00:14:27,554 --> 00:14:31,919
The two intervals overlap across five
point one points, so the honest reading is

321
00:14:31,969 --> 00:14:32,550
a tie.

322
00:14:32,900 --> 00:14:34,600
Next, median latency is fine.

323
00:14:34,650 --> 00:14:38,100
Say instead, report the median and the
slowest three percent.

324
00:14:38,150 --> 00:14:43,000
The median is zero point five seconds, and
the slowest three percent reach seven

325
00:14:43,050 --> 00:14:44,050
point seven seconds.

326
00:14:44,400 --> 00:14:46,423
Next, the groups look the same.

327
00:14:46,473 --> 00:14:51,607
Say instead, put all three panels on one
axis from zero to one hundred percent.

328
00:14:51,657 --> 00:14:55,408
Bars that stood at sixty-three, thirty-
three and forty-five percent become

329
00:14:55,458 --> 00:14:56,790
eighty-nine, ninety and seventy-eight.

330
00:14:57,140 --> 00:14:58,631
Last, the trend looks great.

331
00:14:58,681 --> 00:15:01,714
Say instead, show the whole period,
including the March drop.

332
00:15:01,764 --> 00:15:06,338
The trend shrinks from plus one point
eight to plus zero point five points a

333
00:15:06,387 --> 00:15:06,646
month.

334
00:15:06,696 --> 00:15:09,420
An exact instruction names the choice that
was hiding.

335
00:15:09,770 --> 00:15:12,247
A checklist of six questions before a
chart ships.

336
00:15:12,297 --> 00:15:16,459
First, name the one question the chart
answers, and check it is in the right

337
00:15:16,509 --> 00:15:16,740
family.

338
00:15:16,790 --> 00:15:20,390
If you cannot state it in a sentence, you
cannot pick the chart.

339
00:15:20,740 --> 00:15:23,846
Second, check that the channel matches the
precision needed.

340
00:15:23,896 --> 00:15:25,950
For numbers, choose position or length.

341
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:30,860
Angle, area and volume are less precise,
and color is for telling categories apart.

342
00:15:31,210 --> 00:15:32,677
Third, check the axes.

343
00:15:32,727 --> 00:15:37,229
They should start honestly, run the usual
way, and match across panels.

344
00:15:37,279 --> 00:15:41,780
That covers truncated axes, axis inversion
and inconsistent axes in one line.

345
00:15:42,130 --> 00:15:46,673
Fourth, check that the uncertainty is on
the chart, at the same weight as the

346
00:15:46,723 --> 00:15:48,816
estimate, and that the bar is labeled.

347
00:15:48,866 --> 00:15:52,490
An unlabeled bar could be a standard
deviation or a confidence interval.

348
00:15:52,840 --> 00:15:57,069
Fifth, check that the data measures what
the title says, and put the source inside

349
00:15:57,119 --> 00:15:57,639
the chart.

350
00:15:57,689 --> 00:15:58,781
This is validate first.

351
00:15:58,831 --> 00:16:03,630
A chart built on an unchecked proxy looks
as sure as one built on the real thing.

352
00:16:03,980 --> 00:16:08,571
Sixth, ask whether a reader who did not
build the chart would reach a different

353
00:16:08,621 --> 00:16:08,880
conclusion.

354
00:16:08,930 --> 00:16:10,118
Then say it exactly.

355
00:16:10,168 --> 00:16:13,830
Not make it clear, but zero baseline, one
axis, label the bar.

356
00:16:14,680 --> 00:16:15,259
Let's recap.

357
00:16:15,309 --> 00:16:18,404
A dashboard's layout argues before anyone
takes in a number.

358
00:16:18,454 --> 00:16:21,863
State the question first, then pick
position or length for quantity.

359
00:16:21,913 --> 00:16:25,637
Aleatoric is chance in the data, epistemic
is doubt about the data.

360
00:16:25,687 --> 00:16:29,096
Label every error bar, and draw
uncertainty at equal visual weight.

361
00:16:29,146 --> 00:16:32,555
Truncated, inverted and inconsistent axes
change the story, not the data.

362
00:16:32,605 --> 00:16:35,700
An evidence-grade chart shows what could
prove the claim wrong.

363
00:16:36,050 --> 00:16:37,713
Quiz me on chart terms.

364
00:16:37,763 --> 00:16:42,511
Give me one short case at a time, a chart
described in two sentences.

365
00:16:42,561 --> 00:16:46,965
I must name the exact term and say why its
look-alike is wrong.

366
00:16:47,015 --> 00:16:49,021
Hold the answer until I reply.

367
00:16:49,071 --> 00:16:52,105
Draw on aleatoric uncertainty, epistemic
uncertainty, truncated axes, axis

368
00:16:52,155 --> 00:16:55,189
inversion, inconsistent axes, cherry-
picked window, missing baseline, selective

369
00:16:55,239 --> 00:16:56,560
uncertainty, and false precision.

370
00:16:56,910 --> 00:16:59,377
Key Terms: Visualization Under Validation.

371
00:16:59,427 --> 00:17:01,390
At Nik Bear Brown.
